[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'respective nodes. So, this is the core workflow. All you really have to do is put in a reference photo in the load image node in the middle of the screen here, and it\'ll mask the person by default, but you can use other detector'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'models to mask other areas of a person such as hair, face, eyes, and so on if that\'s the only thing that you are wanting to mask. But, by default, I use a person detector, so it just detects'

[04:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one person, and then after masking and replacing the person that is selected by the detector model, it will replace it with either a Laura that you have selected'

[05:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from the load Laura node here, or if you don\'t have a Laura, you will just need to prompt. You\'ll see here I just have a little note. Prompting is not very necessary for this workflow if you use a'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Laura, but if it struggles to generate your person properly, you can describe as much as you\'d like from physical attributes to props and attire. If you do not use a Laura, you will need to prompt how the person looks at the very least.'

[05:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Otherwise, it will just mask the person that is in the original reference photo and just create any random person that it thinks up because you\'re not really describing'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything or guiding the workflow in any specific way. You can guide the size of the image with this node on screen that I\'m hovering with my cursor right now. This resizes images by the longer side.'

[05:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, let\'s say that you have an image, a base reference photo, that is 1080 pixels as the longer side. So, let\'s say height, for example. This will keep the same exact aspect ratio of the original'

[06:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'photo, but it will upscale it to 1600 pixels instead of 1080. It\'s very important that you keep the original aspect ratio that your reference photo uses. Otherwise, the'

[06:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mask will not properly be stitched onto the reference photo. So, all that really happens after running the workflow is the YOLO detector model will find a bounding box'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around anything labeled person. That detection gets run through this node right here. I have it minimized by default, but I\'m just going to open it.'

[06:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If it will let me. I think it\'s because it\'s pinned. Yeah. So, all of these nodes here, for the most part, you really don\'t need to'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'worry about. I have tweaked and fine-tuned the settings in all of them to the most ideal settings for almost every situation. But, if you\'re using a reference photo where the person is really, really small, like very far in'

[07:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the background or something like that, you\'ll have to change the minimum value in this node right here to zero. The reason that I have it set to 150,000 right now is because let\'s say you have'

[07:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a reference photo of somebody in a crowded area. There\'s a bunch of people behind them, right? Well, if you have this value set to zero, it will detect every single person'

[07:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'within that image. And you don\'t want to do that because then everybody will be getting replaced with whatever you prompt or whatever Laura you have selected. When you set the value to 150,000,'

[07:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it selects the person that is the most close to the camera or center focused. So, that way you\'re only detecting one person. Aside from this node though, there\'s not much to worry about here. Moving over to'

[08:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the in paint crop and K sampler section here, by default I have it set to six steps, a CFG of one, and res 2S is the sampler.'

[08:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The denoise value I use around 0.6 to 0.7. That denoise value is your main creative dial. If you lower it too much, the original image will barely be changed. But if you raise it or keep it'

[08:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around where I have it currently, it will replace the in painted person with your Laura or whatever you have prompted. If you go too high, it will'

[08:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'still replace the person in the original reference photo, but it will probably imagine a bit too much and put them in clothes that don\'t resemble the original reference photo at all.'

[08:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Or something like that. After the workflow runs through the K sampler, it then in paints, it crops, it stitches to the original reference photo\'s background, and then it just runs through the face detailer and the'

[09:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'eye detailer, and you get your output. So, I would like to bring your attention towards this reference photo that I\'m going to be using. Normally, if I were to run this'

[09:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference photo through a image to image workflow, it would probably give me a decent result, but the reason that in painting is so valuable is because there are sometimes where you don\'t want to change the'

[09:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'entire photo. For example, if I run this through an image to image workflow, it will probably just replace the word Coca-Cola with gibberish and the word Fanta with gibberish and all of these brands and'

[09:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'logos and fonts will just get reimagined to the point where it just looks like AI slop. So, when there are people in the background that you want to keep because it looks natural and not AI-like'

[09:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or there\'s brands, logos, text, signs, or whatever it may be that you\'re wishing to preserve, that is where this workflow is great. So, I\'ve just ran the workflow using our reference photo and a'

